What is instruction reordering?
Instruction reordering means the compiler or CPU changes the order in which instructions are executed, while preserving the behavior required by the language and hardware rules.
Why? Performance optimization. The compiler and CPU try to execute instructions efficiently instead of always following the exact source-code order.
Consider:
int a = 0;
int b = 0;

a = 10;  // Statement 1
b = 20;  // Statement 2
The compiler or CPU may arrange these independent operations differently if doing so is safe under the applicable rules.
Rule: as-if-serial
