This distinction is frequently tested in interviews.
Concept	Question it answers	Example
Reordering	Can operations be observed in a different order?	A reader observes ready before the expected effects of another write.
Visibility	Can a thread see another thread's update?	Thread B keeps seeing a stale value.
Atomicity	Can an operation be interrupted partway through?	Two threads increment a counter and lose an update.

For example:
volatile int count = 0;

count++;


Even with volatile, count++ is not an atomic increment. It involves reading the value, incrementing it, and writing it back. Two threads can both read 0 and both write 1, losing one increment.

Use AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet() or another appropriate concurrency mechanism when atomic increments are required.

1. What is double-checked locking?
Double-checked locking is a technique used to create a singleton object lazily while avoiding the cost of acquiring a lock on every access.
- Lazy initialization: Create the object only when it is first needed.
- Thread safety: Ensure multiple threads don't create separate instances.
- Performance: After initialization, avoid synchronizing every call. 

2. First, understand the problem
Suppose you want only one instance of a class.

A naive implementation might be:
class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}


What happens if two threads call getInstance() at almost the same time?
Thread A
instance == null
true
Creates object A


Thread B
instance == null
true
Creates object B



Both threads can pass the check and create separate objects. The implementation is not thread-safe.

We could synchronize the whole method:
public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
    if (instance == null) {
        instance = new Singleton();
    }
    return instance;
}


This is thread-safe, but every call acquires the same monitor, even after the instance exists.

Double-checked locking aims to avoid that repeated synchronization.

How double-checked locking works
There are two checks for instance == null:
1. The first check avoids locking when the instance already exists.
2. The second check, inside the synchronized block, prevents multiple threads from creating the instance simultaneously.
